  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "Bitwise functions reject fractional operands with #NUM! instead of truncating them, while shift counts truncate",
  "statement": "In Excel 16.0 build 20430, the bitwise operand arguments (number, number1, number2) must already be integers in [0, 2^48 - 1]; any fractional part (such as 0.1 or 1.5) returns #NUM! rather than truncating toward zero. By contrast, shift_amount in BITLSHIFT and BITRSHIFT truncates toward zero (e.g. -13.5 becomes -13). All five bitwise functions match 100% of their full-bank rows (5,989 each for BITAND/BITOR/BITXOR; 8,131 for BITLSHIFT; 8,128 for BITRSHIFT).",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured in Excel 16.0 build 20430 W111 bitwise discriminator and held-out batches in CalcFunc-Evidence cache/w111-bitwise-20260929/.",
